<%#
  The import page.

  The strict dialect leads, because it is the one the app can check before it
  writes anything, the one export emits, and the one an AI is asked to produce.
  The lenient formats still work and are still documented, but they are the
  fallback now rather than the headline — the page used to teach `target_uuid`
  and `start_node_uuid` in four worked examples while hiding `schema_version`
  inside a collapsed block, which is backwards.

  Nothing here is transcribed from the models. The step-type table, the field
  lists and the enums live in the generated prompt below, which
  ImportSchemaGenerator derives from StepFieldMap and the VALID_* constants. The
  hand-written copies that used to sit at the bottom of this page had rotted:
  they were missing the `form` step type entirely, named a field `reason` that
  is really `reason_required`, and listed four of the seven resolution types.
%>
<% content_for(:title) { "Import a workflow — TurboFlows" } %>

<div class="page-content">
  <div class="page-main">
    <div class="page-header-section flex items-start justify-between gap-4">
      <div>
        <h1 class="page-header-section__title">Import a workflow</h1>
        <p class="page-header-section__subtitle">
          Upload a file, or have an AI write one. Everything imported lands as a
          draft for you to review — nothing is published until you say so.
        </p>
      </div>
      <%= link_to workflows_path, class: "btn btn--plain btn--circle", aria: { label: "Close" } do %>
        <%= icon "x-mark", class: "icon" %>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <div class="card mb-6">
      <div class="card__body">
        <%= form_with url: workflow_import_path, method: :post, local: true, multipart: true,
                      html: { class: "flex-col-gap-4" },
                      data: { controller: "file-upload", turbo: false } do |form| %>
          <div class="file-dropzone"
               data-file-upload-target="dropZone"
               data-action="dragover->file-upload#dragOver dragleave->file-upload#dragLeave drop->file-upload#drop">
            <div class="file-dropzone__empty" data-file-upload-target="emptyState">
              <%= icon "cloud-arrow-up", class: "icon icon--xl file-dropzone__icon" %>
              <div class="text-sm">
                <label for="file" class="cursor-pointer font-medium">
                  <span>Upload a file</span>
                  <%= form.file_field :file, id: "file", accept: ".json,.csv,.yaml,.yml,.md,.markdown", class: "sr-only", required: true, data: { action: "change->file-upload#fileSelected", file_upload_target: "fileInput" } %>
                </label>
                <span> or drag and drop</span>
              </div>
              <p class="text-xs file-dropzone__hint">
                JSON, CSV, YAML or Markdown, up to
                <%= number_to_human_size(Workflows::ImportsController::MAX_IMPORT_BYTES) %>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="is-hidden file-dropzone__selected" data-file-upload-target="fileSelected">
              <%= icon "check-circle", class: "icon icon--xl file-dropzone__icon" %>
              <p class="font-medium" data-file-upload-target="fileName">No file selected</p>
              <p class="text-xs file-dropzone__hint" data-file-upload-target="fileSize"></p>
              <button type="button" data-action="click->file-upload#clearFile" class="btn btn--plain btn--sm">
                Remove file
              </button>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="flex justify-end gap-3">
            <%= link_to "Cancel", workflows_path, class: "btn btn--plain" %>
            <%= form.submit "Import", class: "btn btn--primary" %>
          </div>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%# The AI path leads. This is the dialect export emits and the only one that
        is checked before anything is written, so it is what the page teaches. %>
    <section class="list-section mb-6">
      <div class="list-section__head">
        <h2 class="list-section__title">Writing a file with an AI</h2>
        <%= link_to "View the JSON Schema", ImportSchemaGenerator::SCHEMA_URL,
                    class: "list-section__link" %>
      </div>

      <div class="card" data-controller="clipboard">
        <div class="card__body">
          <p class="text-sm mb-3">
            A JSON file whose top level carries
            <code>"schema_version": "<%= ImportSchemaGenerator::SCHEMA_VERSION %>"</code>
            takes a strict path: it is checked in full before anything is created,
            and you get a preview or a numbered list of problems rather than a
            half-built workflow. One file may carry up to
            <%= ImportSchemaGenerator::MAX_WORKFLOWS_PER_FILE %> workflows, so a
            linked set imports together and a sub-flow can name another workflow
            in the same file.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm mb-3">
            Paste the block below into whatever agent is writing the file. It is
            generated from the same schema the file is validated against, so it
            cannot describe a format the app no longer accepts.
          </p>
          <div class="flex justify-end mb-2">
            <button type="button" class="btn btn--secondary btn--sm"
                    data-action="click->clipboard#copy" data-clipboard-target="button">
              Copy prompt
            </button>
          </div>
          <pre class="code-block" style="max-height: 24rem; overflow: auto; padding: var(--space-3); background: var(--color-canvas-alt); border-radius: var(--radius); border: 1px solid var(--color-border);"><code data-clipboard-target="source"><%= ImportPromptGenerator.call %></code></pre>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="list-section mb-6">
      <div class="list-section__head">
        <h2 class="list-section__title">What happens when you upload</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="list-section__body">
        <div class="list-row">
          <div class="list-row__body">
            <div class="list-row__title">A strict file — one carrying <code>schema_version</code></div>
            <p class="list-row__sub">
              Nothing is written on upload. You get a preview of every workflow in
              the file, or a report naming each problem, where it is, and what was
              expected. A second confirmation commits the whole file in one
              transaction; if any part of it fails, none of it lands.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="list-row">
          <div class="list-row__body">
            <div class="list-row__title">Any other file</div>
            <p class="list-row__sub">
              Imported straight away, and tolerantly: steps missing required
              fields still land so you can finish them by hand. If anything is
              incomplete, the builder opens with the Health panel showing what
              needs attention.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="list-row">
          <div class="list-row__body">
            <div class="list-row__title">Either way</div>
            <p class="list-row__sub">
              The workflow lands as a private draft and stays one until you
              publish it. Imported drafts are never swept by the draft cleanup, so
              there is no deadline. Graph structure is checked on import —
              unreachable steps, and steps with no path to a Resolve — and again
              on publish, where it has to pass.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="list-row">
          <div class="list-row__body">
            <div class="list-row__title">Where it lands</div>
            <p class="list-row__sub">
              JSON and YAML files can name <code>groups</code>, a <code>folder</code>
              and <code>tags</code>, and the workflow is placed accordingly. CSV
              and Markdown are flat formats that carry none of that, so an import
              from either always lands ungrouped.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <%# Collapsed: still supported, still documented, no longer the headline.
        Each example below was run through WorkflowImporter and imports with no
        errors and no warnings. The four that used to live here did not — every
        one of them transitioned to steps that were not in the file. %>
    <details class="card">
      <summary style="cursor: pointer; padding: var(--space-4);">
        <strong>Other formats: CSV, YAML and Markdown</strong>
        <span class="text-xs" style="color: var(--color-ink-muted);">
          — accepted, but not checked before they are written
        </span>
      </summary>
      <div class="card__body" style="padding-top: 0;">
        <p class="text-sm mb-4">
          These are parsed leniently: unknown fields are ignored, missing ones are
          filled in, and problems surface as warnings after the workflow exists
          rather than as a report before it does. Each example below is complete
          and imports cleanly — the same four-step flow written three ways.
        </p>

        <div class="flex-col-gap-6">
          <div>
            <h3 class="font-medium text-sm mb-1">YAML</h3>
            <p class="text-xs mb-2" style="color: var(--color-ink-muted);">
              Nested arrays for options and transitions. Transitions name their
              target with <code>target_uuid</code>, which is the step's
              <code>id</code> — not the <code>target_id</code> the strict dialect
              uses. Any step can carry a guidance note (<code>help_text</code>)
              and a reference link (<code>reference_url</code>).
            </p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>title: "Password Reset"
description: "Send a customer a password reset link."
tags: ["support", "accounts"]
start_node_uuid: "confirm"
steps:
  - id: "confirm"
    type: question
    title: "Confirm the account"
    question: "Can the customer confirm the email on file?"
    answer_type: yes_no
    variable_name: confirmed
    transitions:
      - target_uuid: "reset"
        condition: "confirmed == 'yes'"
      - target_uuid: "verify"
  - id: "verify"
    type: message
    title: "Verify identity first"
    content: "Ask two security questions before continuing."
    transitions:
      - target_uuid: "reset"
  - id: "reset"
    type: action
    title: "Send the reset link"
    instructions: "Send a password reset link to the address on file."
    help_text: "Never read the link aloud."
    reference_url: "https://kb.example.com/password-reset"
    transitions:
      - target_uuid: "sent"
  - id: "sent"
    type: resolve
    title: "Reset link sent"
    resolution_type: success</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="font-medium text-sm mb-1">CSV</h3>
            <p class="text-xs mb-2" style="color: var(--color-ink-muted);">
              One row per step. <code>type</code> and <code>title</code> are the
              only required columns. Separate multiple transitions with
              semicolons, and write a conditional one as
              <code>target:condition</code>. Options are
              <code>Label:value,Label2:value2</code> or a JSON array. Any step can
              carry a <code>guidance</code> note and a <code>reference_link</code>.
            </p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code>workflow_title,id,type,title,question,answer_type,variable_name,options,instructions,content,resolution_type,guidance,reference_link,transitions
Password Reset,confirm,question,Confirm the account,Can the customer confirm the email on file?,yes_no,confirmed,,,,,,,reset:confirmed == 'yes';verify
,verify,message,Verify identity first,,,,,,Ask two security questions before continuing.,,,,reset
,reset,action,Send the reset link,,,,,Send a password reset link to the address on file.,,,Never read the link aloud.,https://kb.example.com/password-reset,sent
,sent,resolve,Reset link sent,,,,,,,success,,,</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="font-medium text-sm mb-1">Markdown</h3>
            <p class="text-xs mb-2" style="color: var(--color-ink-muted);">
              The H1 is the workflow title and each <code>##</code> heading is a
              step, named by its heading text. Transitions name steps by title.
              A condition must be written <code>(if …)</code> — parentheses
              without the <code>if</code> are read as a transition
              <em>label</em>, which imports without complaint and never branches.
              Any step can carry a <code>Guidance:</code> note and a
              <code>Reference Link:</code>.
            </p>
            <pre class="code-block"><code># Password Reset

Send a customer a password reset link.

## Confirm the account
Type: question
Question: Can the customer confirm the email on file?
Answer Type: yes_no
Variable: confirmed
Transitions: Send the reset link (if confirmed == 'yes'), Verify identity first

## Verify identity first
Type: message
Content: Ask two security questions before continuing.
Transitions: Send the reset link

## Send the reset link
Type: action
Instructions: Send a password reset link to the address on file.
Guidance: Never read the link aloud.
Reference Link: https://kb.example.com/password-reset
Transitions: Reset link sent

## Reset link sent
Type: resolve
Resolution Type: success</code></pre>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="font-medium text-sm mb-1">JSON without <code>schema_version</code></h3>
            <p class="text-xs" style="color: var(--color-ink-muted);">
              Also accepted, and parsed the same lenient way as the formats above:
              transitions use <code>target_uuid</code> and the start step is
              <code>start_node_uuid</code>. Add
              <code>"schema_version": "<%= ImportSchemaGenerator::SCHEMA_VERSION %>"</code>
              to the top level and the file takes the strict path instead, where
              the keys are <code>target_id</code> and <code>start_step_id</code>.
              A workflow exported from TurboFlows is already in the strict form.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</div>
